Key Points to Remember
• Never store plain-text passwords. passwordEncoder.encode() produces a salted one-way
BCrypt hash; the same password gives a different hash each time because of the random salt.
• Login flow: Basic Auth header -> DaoAuthenticationProvider -> CustomUserDetailsService loads the
user from H2 -> PasswordEncoder.matches(plain, hash) -> access granted or 401.
• Roles: .roles("USER") adds the ROLE_ prefix automatically, so store the role without it (USER, not
ROLE_USER); otherwise Spring throws an IllegalArgumentException.
• Changing username: after PUT /api/auth/profile changes the username, use the new username for
later requests.
• Security note: letting a user change their own role is a privilege-escalation risk. It is done here only
because the practical requires it; in real systems roles are changed by an admin.
• H2 in-memory: all registered users disappear when the app restarts. Use
jdbc:h2:file:./data/userdb to keep data between runs.
Conclusion
A database-backed registration and login system was built: passwords are BCrypt-encoded before being
saved in H2, authentication is performed through a custom UserDetailsService and
DaoAuthenticationProvider, and authenticated users can update their profile or deregister their own
account.
